[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: r1’s NAT function (iptables NAT table). Mechanism: PC1 and other inside hosts route Internet-bound traffic toward r1 via OSPF-learned default. r1 forwards those packets out to ISPA, but because there is no source NAT, packets leave with private 10.x source addresses. Upstream networks either drop them or return traffic cannot be associated to a stateful flow, so TCP SYNs time out while internal pings and DNS (resolved by internal servers) continue to work.

Localization and evidence:
- r1 has working ISP connectivity and routing: default via 203.0.113.1 on eth2; r1 successfully pings ISPA (203.0.113.1) and “Internet” IPs (172.253.62.100, 216.239.35.4).
- IP forwarding is enabled on r1 (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1) and filter policies are ACCEPT.
- Critical evidence: r1’s NAT table lacks any MASQUERADE/SNAT rule for inside prefixes; only a DNAT rule for 203.0.113.113→10.5.0.4 is present. Thus, inside-originated Internet traffic is not NATed.

Root cause: Missing/removed source NAT configuration on r1’s WAN egress after a recent change, not an ISP circuit failure.

Corrective fix: Reintroduce source NAT on r1 for inside networks toward both WAN egresses, and make it persistent. Example:
- iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.0.0/8 -o eth2 -j MASQUERADE
- iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.0.0/8 -o eth3 -j MASQUERADE
Optionally prefer ISPA with policy/route tracking, and save rules across reboots. After this, outbound TCP from PC1 to public servers will complete normally without carrier intervention. ISP is not at fault. 
=== END ===